<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · lake</p><h1 class="enc-title">Issyk-Kul</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lake Issyk-Kul</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Issyk-Kul is a large mountain lake without an outlet in north-eastern Kyrgyzstan, lying in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-shan">Tian Shan</a> at about 1,600 metres above sea level. Its basin, sheltered pastureland on the routes between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> and the steppe, was home in turn to the Saka, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wusun">Wusun</a>, the Western Turks and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-khanid-khanate">Karakhanids</a>. It is one of the largest mountain lakes in the world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/issyk-kul.jpg" alt="Issyk-Kul" width="320" height="164" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Қайырқан Әсем · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Issykkul_plyag.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
